# OrionViva — configuration
#
# Copy to `.env` (git-ignored) and edit. Every value below has a working
# default except the passphrase, so a first run needs one line.
#
#     cp .env.example .env
#
# Environment variables always win over this file: an explicit `export` is never
# clobbered, so a shell you set up for one run is not silently overridden.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE SHAPE OF IT, IN ONE PARAGRAPH
#
# Three kinds of thing live in three places, and the split is the whole design.
# YOUR MONEY is encrypted, on your machine, and never leaves it. WHAT THE WORLD
# KNOWS — a bank's line grammar, a brand's category — is impersonal, kept once,
# and shared across every vault you own. WHAT SHIPS WITH THE PACKAGE is the seed
# of that same world knowledge, so a fresh install starts with what other people
# already paid a model to learn. Nothing moves from the second to the third
# without you deciding.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------


# === 1. Your money =========================================================

# Required only for command-line engine development. The desktop asks for this
# in its interface and, on macOS or Windows, protects the default vault's value
# in the operating-system credential store. It is never written into the vault,
# logs, diagnostics, or ordinary application configuration. Moving a vault to
# another device still requires this value.
VIVA_PASSPHRASE=

# Where the encrypted ledger lives. Created on first use if it is not there.
# One vault per person, not per statement or per year — a second vault does not
# know about the first, so balances and net worth would be computed over half
# your money without saying so.
#VIVA_VAULT_DIR=~/.viva-vault

# How to read numbers and dates. The REGION decides the date convention:
# `en-US` reads 03/04 as March 4th, `en-GB` as the 4th of March. Get this wrong
# and every ambiguous date is silently the wrong day. An unrecognised value
# stops the run rather than guessing.
#VIVA_LOCALE=en-US
#VIVA_CURRENCY=USD


# === 2. What the world knows ===============================================
#
# A bank's grammar is the bank's — identical for every customer, and it outlives
# any ledger. "A warehouse club is a warehouse club" is true for everybody.
# None of this is about your money, which is why it sits outside the vault, is
# kept once, and is reused by every vault on this machine.
#
# Two directories, and the split matters:
#
#   LEARNED   what this installation worked out for itself. Writable, and
#             deliberately OUTSIDE any git working tree — a grammar's literal
#             text comes from a model and could carry a name that slipped past
#             the checks, so keeping it where git cannot see it makes committing
#             it impossible rather than merely inadvisable.
#
#   SHIPPED   what travels with the package. Committed, read-only in practice,
#             and read UNDERNEATH the learned data so anything you worked out
#             wins. This is the seed a fresh install starts from.
#
# Promotion from learned to shipped is a deliberate act, never automatic. A
# grammar published there is published to everybody, and the one failure that
# matters — a template that covers 90% of lines while slotting them wrongly —
# is caught by a person reading it and by nothing else.

# Where learned merchant knowledge is kept. Holds `profiles/` and `catalog.json`.
#MERCHANTCORE_HOME=~/.merchantcore

# Older, narrower overrides. Set either and it wins over MERCHANTCORE_HOME for
# that one thing. Useful for pointing a second machine at a catalog you copied,
# or for keeping one vault's experiments apart from your real knowledge.
#VIVA_PROFILES=~/.merchantcore/profiles
#VIVA_CATALOG=~/.merchantcore/catalog.json


# === 3. The model edge =====================================================
#
# Three calls, on three different clocks: once per document you read, four or
# five per vault to learn each bank's grammar, and once per brand nobody has
# identified yet. A second month costs one call per new statement and nothing
# else — everything in between is deterministic and repeats for free.
#
# The key itself is NOT set here. Name the variable that holds it and export
# that separately, so a `.env` accidentally shared carries a pointer rather than
# a credential.

#VIVA_MODEL_ADAPTER=openai-compatible
#VIVA_MODEL=<provider/model-id>
#VIVA_MODEL_BASE_URL=https://<your-provider>/api/v1
#VIVA_MODEL_KEY_ENV=OPENROUTER_API_KEY

# A second, usually smaller model for turning your words into a ruling. Falls
# back to the one above when unset.
#VIVA_INTERPRET_ADAPTER=
#VIVA_INTERPRET_MODEL=
#VIVA_INTERPRET_BASE_URL=
#VIVA_INTERPRET_KEY_ENV=


# === 4. Running it =========================================================

# debug shows every model call, its cost, and which catalog and grammars were
# loaded. Worth turning on the first time: a store that silently loads the wrong
# file looks exactly like a store that is empty.
#VIVA_LOG_LEVEL=info

# How many pages of a document to sample when classifying it.
#VIVA_SAMPLE=3
